WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Welcome to DeadBolt, a free, open
source execution gate from NextEleven.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.000
This walkthrough starts with the problem,
shows the integration choices, and then

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.940
demonstrates real allowed and blocked
effects. DeadBolt gives an operator control

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.720
over a particular agent's next protected
action. It works with a trusted

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.460
dispatcher, whether your application uses
Rust, a local sidecar, an MCP server,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.960
or supported Python function tools. We'll
finish inside the actual Harness executable

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.840
and its web interface. The model
responses in those examples come from

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:42.600
a deterministic local fixture. The files,
operator commands, admission decisions,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.500
and tool execution are real. Cedar
provides the AI generated narration. The

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.080
visual presentation is an educational
demo, separate from the shipping product

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:53.520
interface.

00:00:53.882 --> 00:00:59.662
Once an agent can write files,
call services, or send messages, a

00:00:59.662 --> 00:01:05.002
bad decision can become a real change.
Untrusted material can influence what

00:01:05.002 --> 00:01:10.482
the model proposes. OpenAI's current
agent documentation recommends putting

00:01:10.482 --> 00:01:15.722
validation next to the tool that creates
the side effect. OWASP's excessive

00:01:15.722 --> 00:01:21.522
agency guidance recommends minimum permissions,
approval for high impact actions, and

00:01:21.522 --> 00:01:26.902
authorization of every downstream request.
Those are engineering reasons to establish

00:01:26.902 --> 00:01:32.042
an execution boundary. They do not
establish a universal legal requirement to

00:01:32.042 --> 00:01:37.662
install DeadBolt. Your application,
contracts, and jurisdiction determine

00:01:37.662 --> 00:01:43.462
any specific obligation. DeadBolt supplies
one deterministic control in a larger

00:01:43.462 --> 00:01:49.162
design. Combine it with narrow tools, real
service permissions, schema validation,

00:01:49.702 --> 00:01:53.022
and operating system isolation
appropriate to your environment.

00:01:53.483 --> 00:01:59.363
The model proposes work. Your trusted
application validates the arguments and assigns

00:01:59.363 --> 00:02:04.023
the run identity and routed tool name.
DeadBolt checks the current lease

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:08.803
and policy. Your dispatcher starts
the body only after an explicit allow.

00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:15.003
Every other result blocks the protected
action. Operators manage policy, approval,

00:02:15.303 --> 00:02:19.843
and revocation through a separate control
path. These controls should never be

00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:25.823
model tools. The dispatcher, database,
operator credentials, and routing

00:02:25.823 --> 00:02:30.083
remain trusted. A caller that
can skip the gate or change its

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:35.003
store is outside this boundary. DeadBolt
is not an operating system sandbox,

00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:39.903
and it does not shut down a frontier
model. It refuses subsequent

00:02:39.903 --> 00:02:44.403
protected admissions. It also cannot
cancel a tool body that is already

00:02:44.403 --> 00:02:44.653
running.

00:02:44.969 --> 00:02:50.677
Choose the version before choosing an
integration example. The published version, one

00:02:50.677 --> 00:02:55.829
point zero point three, supports the
established lease gate, native CLI, sidecar,

00:02:56.249 --> 00:03:01.989
and MCP proxy. Candidate examples
add callback helpers, an installable

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:07.749
Python client, the optional SDK adapter,
scoped credentials, and exact action

00:03:07.749 --> 00:03:12.929
approval. Those additions are not yet in
the published archives. Native downloads

00:03:12.929 --> 00:03:18.909
avoid a Rust compiler requirement. Select
your operating system and processor, verify

00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:24.609
the checksum, unpack the archive,
and run version and drill. A source

00:03:24.609 --> 00:03:29.589
install needs Rust and the platform's
C compiler or build tools. The

00:03:29.589 --> 00:03:34.009
candidate should be pinned to its
full reviewed commit. At this review,

00:03:34.229 --> 00:03:39.269
no crates dot io or PyPI package
release was available. Use the

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:42.049
installation guide instead of
assuming registry commands work.

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:48.802
The fastest evaluation uses a local
sidecar and a temporary file dispatcher.

00:03:49.042 --> 00:03:53.442
It needs Python's standard library and
the native executable, with no model

00:03:53.442 --> 00:03:59.022
account. Run the evaluation script
with the executable path. The script

00:03:59.022 --> 00:04:04.902
provisions isolated state, configures policy,
tries actions, and checks whether each

00:04:04.902 --> 00:04:10.342
file actually exists. Here the permitted
write succeeds. An off policy tool

00:04:10.342 --> 00:04:15.822
is refused. An irreversible action requires
approval, uses it once, and refuses

00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:21.082
a replay. Parent revocation also blocks
the registered child, while an unrelated

00:04:21.082 --> 00:04:26.502
run continues. Stopping the sidecar
blocks the next action, and restarting it

00:04:26.502 --> 00:04:30.602
preserves revocation. These eleven checks
passed with the published archive and the

00:04:30.602 --> 00:04:35.502
candidate binary. They verify this
dispatcher and these effects; they are not

00:04:35.502 --> 00:04:37.262
proof of arbitrary agent containment.

00:04:37.665 --> 00:04:42.125
In Rust, add the DeadBolt
crate from a released tag or a

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:47.365
reviewed full revision. The package
name is n eleven deadbolt, and the

00:04:47.365 --> 00:04:52.225
library name is deadbolt. Create a
gate with enforcement enabled and fail

00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:57.485
closed, using a private state directory.
In trusted setup, create the run

00:04:57.485 --> 00:05:02.925
lease and configure the actual tool policy.
Candidate dispatch helpers accept the

00:05:02.925 --> 00:05:07.905
real callback. They check immediately before
invoking it, preserve its return value,

00:05:07.905 --> 00:05:12.525
and never invoke it on a denial.
Async dispatch checks when the

00:05:12.525 --> 00:05:17.485
future is polled, rather than when it
is constructed. Existing published APIs

00:05:17.485 --> 00:05:22.305
can use a manual admit followed
immediately by the body. Protect every

00:05:22.305 --> 00:05:26.485
effect route. Do not cache an
allow for a session, or spend

00:05:26.485 --> 00:05:28.205
a one shot on a separate precheck.

00:05:28.437 --> 00:05:33.457
The local sidecar exposes admission over
a Unix socket or authenticated loopback

00:05:33.457 --> 00:05:39.037
TCP. The Python client uses only the
standard library; the Node client

00:05:39.037 --> 00:05:44.277
uses built in modules. Configure matching
database and evidence paths for server

00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:49.317
and operator, and the endpoint for
the dispatcher. Start the sidecar and

00:05:49.317 --> 00:05:54.117
create the lease through trusted setup
before dispatch. The candidate helper returns

00:05:54.117 --> 00:05:59.437
an execution flag, the decision, and
the callback result. A blocked action

00:05:59.437 --> 00:06:04.177
has no callback effect. The Python
wheel does not bundle or start

00:06:04.177 --> 00:06:08.497
the executable. Build from source
with Python three point ten or later;

00:06:09.257 --> 00:06:13.217
the base wheel runs on three point
nine or later. Async applications

00:06:13.217 --> 00:06:18.017
use the async helper. Every protected
application route still needs its own

00:06:18.017 --> 00:06:18.267
wrapper.

00:06:18.433 --> 00:06:24.153
For a stdio MCP integration, launch
the server through DeadBolt's MCP

00:06:24.153 --> 00:06:29.633
proxy with an executor assigned agent
identity. The proxy checks each routed

00:06:29.633 --> 00:06:35.333
tools call before forwarding it. Operator
control stays outside the MCP tool

00:06:35.333 --> 00:06:40.733
list. You can use embedded state or
the sidecar route. Scoped admission

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:45.773
credentials require the sidecar mode. The
child does not inherit either DeadBolt

00:06:45.773 --> 00:06:51.233
bearer token. The candidate bounds frames
and responses and handles child termination

00:06:51.233 --> 00:06:56.473
and client disconnects. These checks
do not cover arbitrary server startup,

00:06:57.273 --> 00:07:02.773
every other MCP method, or background
work inside the server. Protect and

00:07:02.773 --> 00:07:08.153
validate the server implementation itself.
The acceptance suite exercises a real MCP

00:07:08.153 --> 00:07:13.053
child. For an existing host, begin
with one narrow tool route and

00:07:13.053 --> 00:07:16.233
prove allowed and denied effects
before expanding coverage.

00:07:16.586 --> 00:07:21.986
The optional Python adapter protects
actual function tools in the tested

00:07:21.986 --> 00:07:27.926
OpenAI Agents SDK. Replace the function
tool decorator with the protected

00:07:27.926 --> 00:07:33.266
tool decorator on each effect function.
The SDK still builds its schema,

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:37.786
validates arguments, and runs its own
guardrails and human approval lifecycle.

00:07:39.146 --> 00:07:43.626
DeadBolt performs admission when the protected
body runs, including after a reviewed

00:07:43.626 --> 00:07:48.506
run resumes. The adapter does not
create a lease, grant operator approval,

00:07:48.786 --> 00:07:53.286
or protect hosted tools automatically.
It reports a denial as a typed

00:07:53.286 --> 00:07:58.146
cause through the SDK runner, so
applications can display the decision without

00:07:58.146 --> 00:08:03.846
parsing prose. Installed wheel acceptance
exercised nineteen cases using the real

00:08:03.846 --> 00:08:09.406
runner and sidecar without provider
requests. The tested SDK revision

00:08:09.406 --> 00:08:12.646
is pinned; review compatibility
before changing it.

00:08:12.812 --> 00:08:16.812
A lease belongs to an agent
identity, not a vendor API key.

00:08:17.772 --> 00:08:22.272
Give each run an identity in trusted
infrastructure. The default lease lifetime

00:08:22.272 --> 00:08:28.232
is sixty seconds. A live admission, including
ordinary denials, refreshes the sliding

00:08:28.232 --> 00:08:33.892
lease. Silence allows it to expire.
Killed or expired leases do not

00:08:33.892 --> 00:08:38.292
slide, and a protected tool wrapper
must not silently renew an expired

00:08:38.292 --> 00:08:43.952
identity. Explicit ensure belongs in
trusted setup, not in model control or

00:08:43.952 --> 00:08:48.872
a retry loop. Action deadlines and
admission credential deadlines are fixed and

00:08:48.872 --> 00:08:53.592
do not slide. A fresh run needs
deliberate trusted setup and policy.

00:08:54.732 --> 00:08:58.912
Track these three lifetimes separately.
This makes it possible to stop a

00:08:58.912 --> 00:09:03.172
particular workflow without revoking a
shared model key or interrupting unrelated

00:09:03.172 --> 00:09:03.812
applications.

00:09:04.351 --> 00:09:10.291
Pause temporarily blocks all protected
admissions for one agent. Clip blocks

00:09:10.291 --> 00:09:15.531
a particular tool for that agent.
Resume clears pause and clips, while

00:09:15.531 --> 00:09:20.651
preserving the configured policy. Kill
revokes the agent and its registered

00:09:20.651 --> 00:09:25.931
descendants. Resume does not resurrect
a kill. Choose pause when you need

00:09:25.931 --> 00:09:31.131
to investigate, clip when a single
capability should stop, and kill when

00:09:31.131 --> 00:09:35.131
the run must remain revoked. Here
a paused file callback does not

00:09:35.131 --> 00:09:40.271
run. After resume, a permitted callback
writes a file. Clipping the write

00:09:40.271 --> 00:09:45.431
tool refuses another attempt. These are
fresh sidecar decisions and observed callback

00:09:45.431 --> 00:09:50.011
effects. A control issued after an
action has already been admitted cannot

00:09:50.011 --> 00:09:55.271
undo that action. Operators still need
cancellation and recovery mechanisms in their

00:09:55.271 --> 00:09:55.751
application.

00:09:56.115 --> 00:10:00.835
Policy limits the tool names an agent
can use, the declared destination

00:10:00.835 --> 00:10:06.315
tokens it can reach, and caller
reported spend. Unset lists remain open,

00:10:06.795 --> 00:10:11.535
so set narrow policies intentionally.
A tool outside the list is refused

00:10:11.535 --> 00:10:16.875
with purpose exceeded. A foreign host
is also refused. With a destination

00:10:16.875 --> 00:10:21.915
list, a network class tool missing
its host is refused. Local tools

00:10:21.915 --> 00:10:26.855
without a destination are treated differently.
Destination tokens must be derived

00:10:26.855 --> 00:10:31.215
from the route the body really
uses. DeadBolt does not enforce DNS,

00:10:31.735 --> 00:10:37.335
redirects, network sockets, or a filesystem
path jail. Spending is reported by

00:10:37.335 --> 00:10:41.735
trusted infrastructure; this is not
a billing meter or an atomic purchase

00:10:41.735 --> 00:10:47.475
reservation. Crossing a configured cap
stops further protected admissions. Each

00:10:47.475 --> 00:10:52.015
child requires its own policy, configured
in trusted setup before dispatch.

00:10:52.276 --> 00:10:57.996
The existing irreversible tool policy
requires an operator one shot. An

00:10:57.996 --> 00:11:03.616
unapproved attempt returns needs human. The
operator approves the specific agent and

00:11:03.616 --> 00:11:08.696
tool. The next admitted call consumes
that approval, and another attempt needs

00:11:08.696 --> 00:11:14.136
review again. This simple mechanism is
useful when the trusted application controls

00:11:14.136 --> 00:11:18.736
the effect. Its scope is the
agent and tool pair. It does

00:11:18.736 --> 00:11:24.136
not bind the recipient, message text,
amount, or other arguments. Do not

00:11:24.136 --> 00:11:28.876
describe this broad permission as
approval of an exact message. For effects

00:11:28.876 --> 00:11:33.876
where those values matter, use the
candidate exact action path. Keep review

00:11:33.876 --> 00:11:39.776
outside model control, and consume the
approval only at execution. Declining a

00:11:39.776 --> 00:11:43.756
separate application review should not
burn a permission before the body is

00:11:43.756 --> 00:11:44.536
eligible to run.

00:11:44.935 --> 00:11:50.475
Exact action approval binds the
executor's run, routed tool, destination,

00:11:51.235 --> 00:11:56.655
validated JSON arguments, a unique
nonce, and a fixed deadline. Prepare the

00:11:56.655 --> 00:12:01.655
review request in trusted code, and
protect the private review file. Inspect

00:12:01.655 --> 00:12:06.355
the actual content and its fingerprint.
The operator approves that reviewed

00:12:06.355 --> 00:12:11.275
fingerprint. The dispatch helper later
uses a copied argument snapshot for the

00:12:11.275 --> 00:12:16.135
body. It must not read different
recipients or amounts from mutable state.

00:12:17.155 --> 00:12:21.535
Changing reviewed content does not
match the grant and is refused. The

00:12:21.535 --> 00:12:27.235
original reviewed write succeeds once; its
replay is refused. This demonstration uses

00:12:27.235 --> 00:12:32.395
a temporary file transport, not email.
Rebuild a request with every effect

00:12:32.395 --> 00:12:37.415
affecting value resolved, and version
your tool identity when its schema or

00:12:37.415 --> 00:12:37.935
behavior changes.

00:12:38.201 --> 00:12:42.781
The version one fingerprint uses
SHA two fifty six over a domain

00:12:42.781 --> 00:12:48.541
separated RFC eight seven eight five
canonical JSON envelope. Objects,

00:12:49.121 --> 00:12:54.901
numbers, Unicode, and request limits have
explicit rules. Duplicate keys, unknown

00:12:54.901 --> 00:13:00.581
fields, invalid numbers, and oversized
envelopes are refused. Use the

00:13:00.581 --> 00:13:06.201
dedicated action parser at raw JSON
boundaries. For money, use safe integer

00:13:06.201 --> 00:13:11.601
minor units or decimal strings rather
than ambiguous precision. The grant is

00:13:11.601 --> 00:13:16.821
consumed atomically with primary SQLite
evidence. That does not make an external

00:13:16.821 --> 00:13:21.661
effect exactly once. A crash or tool
error can leave completion unknown

00:13:21.661 --> 00:13:27.261
after consumption. Reconcile the application
outcome and use downstream idempotency

00:13:27.261 --> 00:13:33.061
before seeking a new approval. Grant
revocation cannot cancel running work. These

00:13:33.061 --> 00:13:36.461
limits are part of the action approval
guide and the upgrade procedure.

00:13:36.747 --> 00:13:40.967
The candidate separates a workload's
admission credential from the operator token.

00:13:41.947 --> 00:13:46.247
Trusted setup provisions the lease and
policy, then issues a private credential

00:13:46.247 --> 00:13:50.807
file for one agent with a fixed
expiry. The workload can request

00:13:50.807 --> 00:13:56.287
legacy or exact admission for that
identity. It cannot create agents, change

00:13:56.287 --> 00:14:01.847
policy, inspect other agents, grant approvals,
or report spend. Remove the operator

00:14:01.847 --> 00:14:06.827
token from workload environments. Here a
matching credential admits the reviewed

00:14:06.827 --> 00:14:12.527
write, while another identity is refused.
A control request is forbidden, and

00:14:12.527 --> 00:14:18.107
credential revocation blocks another callback.
SQLite stores a credential hash, while

00:14:18.107 --> 00:14:23.247
the bearer stays private. Socket access
alone no longer grants anonymous authority

00:14:23.247 --> 00:14:28.187
after the first credential is issued.
Protect the database and CLI with

00:14:28.187 --> 00:14:32.887
operating system ownership; a same
user process is not isolated by these

00:14:32.887 --> 00:14:33.137
headers.

00:14:34.176 --> 00:14:39.816
Register a child under its parent
before starting its protected work. Then

00:14:39.816 --> 00:14:45.556
explicitly configure the child's policy.
Registration creates lineage; it does not

00:14:45.556 --> 00:14:51.536
copy the parent's tool or destination
restrictions. Killing a parent revokes its

00:14:51.536 --> 00:14:56.136
registered descendants. It does not revoke
an independent identity elsewhere in the

00:14:56.136 --> 00:15:01.136
same store. The file evaluation shows
the killed parent's callback blocked,

00:15:01.936 --> 00:15:07.656
the child's callback blocked, and an
unrelated agent still writing. Harness

00:15:07.656 --> 00:15:12.296
also binds its spawned agents and
swarm tasks to this lineage, with

00:15:12.296 --> 00:15:17.876
cancellation behavior owned by Harness.
DeadBolt alone controls subsequent

00:15:17.876 --> 00:15:23.716
admission, rather than proving universal
process tree termination. Keep the trusted

00:15:23.716 --> 00:15:28.656
executor responsible for identities,
child registration, and complete dispatch

00:15:28.656 --> 00:15:33.936
coverage. A model that chooses fresh
identities can undermine a poorly designed

00:15:33.936 --> 00:15:34.376
integration.

00:15:35.438 --> 00:15:41.108
DeadBolt records admission attempts
and operator changes in SQLite and JSON

00:15:41.108 --> 00:15:46.038
Lines evidence. Observed facts and
inferred conclusions remain distinct.

00:15:47.258 --> 00:15:52.918
Content identifiers hash the recorded payload;
inferred rows cite their premises. The

00:15:52.918 --> 00:15:57.698
incident command exports an agent's
decision history, current policy, revocation

00:15:57.698 --> 00:16:03.038
time, and registered children. These
records do not identify the human who

00:16:03.038 --> 00:16:07.678
held a token, prove that an external
operation completed, or certify legal

00:16:07.678 --> 00:16:12.918
compliance. Generated narratives are not
evidence rows. The candidate's action

00:16:12.918 --> 00:16:17.558
records contain metadata and fingerprints
rather than raw arguments, but private

00:16:17.558 --> 00:16:22.438
review files still contain the actual
content. Protect exports and retention

00:16:22.438 --> 00:16:27.418
appropriately. Optional Witness integration
is a separate sink with its own failure

00:16:27.418 --> 00:16:32.998
boundary. Use these records to investigate,
then reconcile against application and

00:16:32.998 --> 00:16:33.818
provider evidence.

00:16:35.316 --> 00:16:40.276
A dispatcher should run the body
only on a fresh explicit allow.

00:16:41.416 --> 00:16:47.376
An unavailable sidecar, malformed response,
timeout, or authentication failure must

00:16:47.376 --> 00:16:53.136
not become permission. Enable enforcement
and fail closed. Here stopping the

00:16:53.136 --> 00:16:58.836
sidecar prevents a file effect. Restarting
it preserves the killed identity, while

00:16:58.836 --> 00:17:04.616
the unrelated lease remains active. Keep
database and evidence paths consistent, use

00:17:04.616 --> 00:17:10.396
private state directories, and bind locally.
The repository includes container and

00:17:10.396 --> 00:17:15.956
systemd guidance, but your target
deployment still needs acceptance. Back up

00:17:15.956 --> 00:17:21.616
SQLite consistently and test restoration
with dispatch stopped. Candidate migration

00:17:21.616 --> 00:17:26.616
adds tables. Older binaries do not
enforce the new credential or exact

00:17:26.616 --> 00:17:31.976
action boundaries, so rollback needs
a reviewed recovery plan. Primary

00:17:31.976 --> 00:17:37.816
SQLite transactions do not make JSON
Lines, Witness, and external effects one

00:17:37.816 --> 00:17:38.636
global transaction.

00:17:39.198 --> 00:17:44.698
Now we're using the optimized Harness
executable copied outside its source checkout.

00:17:45.578 --> 00:17:50.678
Its explicit demo configuration enables
DeadBolt and fail closed, with isolated

00:17:50.678 --> 00:17:55.618
state. A synthetic loopback provider
proposes a normal file write.

00:17:56.798 --> 00:18:00.998
Harness runs the protected tool, and
the file appears. Before the next

00:18:00.998 --> 00:18:05.918
proposed write, the operator kills that
run. Harness reports a killed denial,

00:18:06.198 --> 00:18:11.198
and the second file does not exist.
A different Harness agent then

00:18:11.198 --> 00:18:15.498
writes its own file successfully.
The output on screen is captured from

00:18:15.498 --> 00:18:21.438
actual commands and tool results, with
demo paths shortened for readability. This

00:18:21.438 --> 00:18:25.538
is a deterministic integration test,
not a claim about a particular model's

00:18:25.538 --> 00:18:30.998
behavior. Harness currently uses legacy
admission and correctly refuses exact only

00:18:30.998 --> 00:18:34.218
tools until a dedicated exact
action adapter is added.

00:18:34.411 --> 00:18:39.471
Developers can also work through Harness's
actual web interface. This screenshot

00:18:39.471 --> 00:18:44.291
comes from the running application, not
a proposed DeadBolt dashboard. We asked

00:18:44.291 --> 00:18:47.831
the local fixture to read a
harmless document and then attempt a

00:18:47.831 --> 00:18:51.791
write. The read went through the
permitted tool. The write was refused

00:18:51.791 --> 00:18:56.351
with purpose exceeded, and the output
file was independently checked to be

00:18:56.351 --> 00:19:01.531
absent. Sessions, the conversation,
and activity events remain visible.

00:19:01.531 --> 00:19:06.111
A done event means the tool
call returned; it does not by

00:19:06.111 --> 00:19:10.611
itself mean the requested effect succeeded.
The application should expose the

00:19:10.611 --> 00:19:16.271
decision clearly. DeadBolt's operator
control is still separate from the chat. This

00:19:16.271 --> 00:19:20.831
demo interface uses the same protected
execution boundary, with a deterministic

00:19:20.831 --> 00:19:22.951
provider and dedicated demo data.

00:19:23.172 --> 00:19:28.032
The review found a meaningful integration
bug. Harness admitted a tool before

00:19:28.032 --> 00:19:32.552
waiting for human review. After
review, it checked only stored kill or

00:19:32.552 --> 00:19:37.212
pause state. A changed policy or
expired lease could therefore be missed,

00:19:37.712 --> 00:19:42.132
and declining review could consume
a one shot too early. Four new

00:19:42.132 --> 00:19:46.792
regressions failed against that behavior.
The correction performs the admission once,

00:19:46.792 --> 00:19:52.472
after review, immediately before the registered
body or reviewed content write. Seven

00:19:52.472 --> 00:19:57.872
new regression cases now cover these paths,
including operator kill, clipped writes,

00:19:58.412 --> 00:20:03.717
approval preservation, and exact only
refusal. The full workspace passed eight

00:20:03.717 --> 00:20:09.172
hundred twenty five tests; the focused
optimized suites passed two hundred. All

00:20:09.172 --> 00:20:13.672
fifteen remote checks passed. These are
review results for the pinned candidate,

00:20:13.672 --> 00:20:17.132
with unmerged pull requests
and no new release yet.

00:20:17.385 --> 00:20:22.165
Start with one narrow tool route in
your application. Choose the released

00:20:22.165 --> 00:20:27.405
or reviewed candidate API, configure a
private store, assign a stable identity,

00:20:27.885 --> 00:20:33.125
and set a useful policy. Wrap the
actual effect function and test

00:20:33.125 --> 00:20:38.165
its allowed behavior. Then verify
policy denial, operator revocation,

00:20:39.145 --> 00:20:44.605
expiry, approval replay, and gate outage
without accepting a hidden fallback. Add

00:20:44.605 --> 00:20:49.645
exact content review when arguments
determine a sensitive outcome. Add scoped

00:20:49.645 --> 00:20:55.045
credentials when workloads should lack
operator authority. Record which routes are

00:20:55.045 --> 00:20:59.745
covered, and expand only after their
effects are checked. DeadBolt is free

00:20:59.745 --> 00:21:04.225
under Apache two point zero, with no
model subscription required for the

00:21:04.225 --> 00:21:09.985
gate. The guide, reproducible fixtures,
source revisions, captions, and

00:21:09.985 --> 00:21:15.645
evidence accompany this walkthrough. Your
next step is a small independently verified

00:21:15.645 --> 00:21:16.105
integration.
